data_processing.ipynb:
1. Natural torpor loading and cleaning
2. Synthetic torpor
· For each mouse (one sheet per RFID):
Extracts baseline period (first 24 h) and CNO (synthetic torpor) period (next 24 h)
3. Natural vs synthetic comparison: Combines natural table 'df_nat' and synthetic table 'df_syn'

FFt.py:
frequency-domain analysis into reusable functions
· Load a temperature time series for a given mouse and window
· Compute residuals and FFT

Temperature threshold:
Records the temperature-only threshold for torpor
· Uses all natural torpor mice (N9, N10, N11, N12, N14, N15, N16)
· Compares baseline temperature distributions to torpor bouts
· Derives a cut-off around 34°C

N15: temperature without torpor:
About N15 baseline (no torpor) daily curve
· Takes a long baseline segment for N15 (days with no torpor influence)
· Fits a 24-h sinusoid
Plots: Hourly mean temperature vs time-of-day

N15: temperature with torpor：
1. Global view plot
· Baseline daily oscillations above ~36–38°C
· Deep torpor drop to ~24°C after fasting
· Recovery to baseline levels
2. Zoomed torpor bout with exponential fits
Plots:
· Blue dots: real data
· Blue/orange curves: exponential fits

SVM: where it turns all the physiological features (temperature, Fourier index, activity) into an automatic classifier using Support Vector Machines
Load a feature matrix (one row per mouse or per bout) with:
· Fourier indices
· Exponential cooling/warming parameters
· Tmin, torpor duration

Comparing parameters.py: a parameter comparison toolbox
Plot:
· Scatterplots between parameters: e.g. Tmin vs weight, etc.
· Group comparisons: torpor vs non-torpor mice, male vs female, light vs heavy
